/-!
# Right maximality in the symplectic row of 5.2.8

For K = O²(H), the actual symplectic amalgam comparison identifies O₂(K)
with an elementary group of order 2^(3n), generated by [O₂(K),K]. Every
local overcomponent J has the same core and acts faithfully on it. The
binary embedding theorem forces n = 2 for a proper overcomponent. Orders
exclude J₁, PSL₂(25), and the exceptional prime PSL₂ rows. For the triple
cover of A₇, the central cubic supplies a faithful irreducible submodule;
the proved small-degree exclusion applies. Thus J/O₂(J) is the actual A₇.

The original natural-block interval and the simple A₇ quotient imply that
J is short and U(J) = O₂(J), of order 64. The checked binary A₇ spectrum
leaves module dimension four or six. The exceptional four-dimensional
block has U of order sixteen, while the ordinary six-dimensional block
cannot contain the normalized natural L₂(4) block. Both cases contradict
the original core and inclusion. This proves K maximal, including Sp₄(4).

The final normalizer application discharges maximality, common-Sylow and
both enlarged-core premises. The Chapter12 extension applies it to the
actual whole-residual pair, retaining both compatible orientations and
S = O₂(N_G(L) ∩ H). Nonblock maximality, exhaustive F.1.12 comparison,
original uniqueness and ambient completion recognition remain obligations.
Source: Aschbacher–Smith 5.2.8, A.3.12–14, G.7.3 and C.5.13, printed
pp.652,47–50,348–349,163 (`refs/quasithin.pdf`).
-/

